ec2 purchansing optinons
------------------------
1) On-Demand Instances
        ✅ Definition:

        Pay for compute capacity per second/hour with no long-term commitment. we terminate or Provisioned when we want. so it most felxible type.

        ✅ Use Cases:
            Testing
            Short-term workloads
            Unpredictable traffic
        ✅ Pros:
            Flexible
        No upfront cost
        ❌ Cons:
            Expensive compared to others

2) Reserved Instances (RI)
✅ Definition:

        Reserve capacity for 1 or 3 years to get discounted pricing. uy this type of instance, bwe are responsible for the entire price ragardless how often we use it.

        ✅ Use Cases:
        Long-running applications
        Stable workloads
        ✅ Pros:
        Up to ~70% cheaper
        ❌ Cons:
        Less flexibility
        Commitment required
3) Spot Instances

✅ Definition:

        Use unused AWS capacity at very low cost (up to 90% discount).

        ✅ Use Cases:
        Batch jobs
        Data processing
        CI/CD pipelines
        ✅ Pros:
        Very cheap 💰
        ❌ Cons:
        Can be terminated anytime ⚠️
4) Dedicated Instances / Hosts
✅ Definition:

        Physical servers dedicated only to your account.

        ✅ Use Cases:
        Compliance requirements
        Licensing constraints
        ✅ Pros:
        Full isolation
        ❌ Cons:
        Very expensive
        🔥 Which is Commonly Used?

        👉 Most commonly used in real-time:

✅ On-Demand → Default choice (most common)
✅ Reserved → For production long-term apps
✅ Spot → For cost optimization

👉 ❌ Dedicated → Rare (only special cases)

🔥 Real-Time Usage Pattern:
Dev/Test → On-Demand
Production → Reserved
Batch jobs → Spot
✅ Interview One-Liner:

EC2 offers On-Demand, Reserved, Spot, and Dedicated instances, where On-Demand is most commonly used for flexibility, Reserved for cost savings in long-term workloads, and Spot for low-cost non-critical workloads.



instane type and family
-----------------------
    Instance family: is a category of EC2 instances designed for a specific type of workload.
            | Family            | Example | Use Case        |
            | ----------------- | ------- | --------------- |
            | General Purpose   | t3, m5  | Balanced apps   |
            | Compute Optimized | c5      | CPU-heavy apps  |
            | Memory Optimized  | r5      | Databases       |
            | Storage Optimized | i3      | High disk usage |
            | GPU               | p3      | AI/ML           |
    Instance type is the specific size/configuration within a family.
    👉 It defines:
        vCPU
        RAM
        Network performance

